      "type": "about",
      "tabLabel": "About",
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "5 Card Omaha",
          "subtitle": "Card Game",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "5 Card Omaha: five hole cards each, five community cards. Use exactly two hole and three board for best five-card hand. Pot Limit or No Limit. 2-9 players."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Omaha poker is a modern casino variant that emerged in the USA in the 1980s, quickly spreading through card rooms and tournaments. Five Card Omaha (Big O) extends standard four-card Omaha by dealing five private cards to each player while keeping the \"exactly two hole and three board\" rule."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Setup",
          "subtitle": "Table, deck, equipment, and deal",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Two or more players."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Standard 52-card deck"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "52-card deck, chips"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Five hole cards each; five community cards (flop, turn, river)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Variations",
          "subtitle": "Known ways this game changes",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "style": "unordered",
              "items": [
                {
                  "text": "Five Card Omaha High: Standard version; only the best high hand wins the pot. Pot Limit or No Limit."
                },
                {
                  "text": "Five Card Omaha Hi-Low 8-or-Better: Pot splits between best high and best qualifying low (8-high or better); each hand uses exactly two hole and three board. Commonly fixed limit."
                },
                {
                  "text": "Pot Limit vs No Limit: Pot Limit: maximum bet or raise equals current pot size. No Limit: any amount up to stack."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
